<Layout title="5.4 Location">
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/ibbm/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <a href="/ibbm/unit5/">Unit 5</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <span>5.4 Location</span>
  </div>
  <div class="topic-hero">
    <span class="topic-number">5.4</span>
    <h1>Location</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Why a business locates where it does, from the factors behind a single site to the international question of outsourcing, offshoring and reshoring.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tabs">
    <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="switchTab(this,'notes')">Notes</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'worked')">Worked Examples</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'practice')">Practice</button>
  </div>

  <!-- NOTES -->
  <div id="notes" class="tab-content active">

    <div class="learning-goals">
      <div class="learning-goals-title">Learning Goals</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Define location, and distinguish it from place</li>
        <li>Use STEEPLE to think about a location decision</li>
        <li>Justify a location decision with reference to a business's specific needs</li>
        <li>Explain clustering and its effect on business location</li>
        <li>Define outsourcing/subcontracting, insourcing, offshoring, and reshoring</li>
        <li>Distinguish between outsourcing and offshoring, recognising a business can do both at once</li>
        <li>Explain why a business chooses to outsource, offshore, insource or reshore production</li>
        <li>Evaluate outsourcing, offshoring, insourcing and reshoring as methods of reorganising production, nationally and internationally</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Location vs Place</h2>
      <p>These two terms get mixed up constantly. Know which one a question is using before you answer.</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Location</th><th>Place</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>The physical site where the business runs its operations. Chosen using the STEEPLE factors: cost of land/rent, workforce, transport, incentives. An <strong>operations</strong> decision.</td>
            <td>The "P" of the marketing mix: distribution. How the product reaches the customer — online, wholesaler, or retail network. A <strong>marketing mix</strong> decision.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="callout key-term">
        <div class="callout-title">Where they overlap</div>
        <p>For a hotel or a retail store, the premises <em>is</em> both the site of operations <strong>and</strong> the point of customer access. In that case location and place are the same physical decision, but you should still be able to say which function's objectives it is serving.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">The STEEPLE framework for location</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Factor</th><th>What to consider</th><th>Type</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Sociocultural</strong></td><td>Target market culture and demographics, plus community ties and quality of life for staff and managers</td><td>Qualitative</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Technological</strong></td><td>Transport networks, remote work, e-commerce</td><td>Mixed</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Economic</strong></td><td>Land and rent costs, labour cost and skill, supplier access, clustering</td><td>Mostly quantitative</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Environmental / Ethical</strong></td><td>Exploitation risk versus positive community and environmental impact</td><td>Qualitative</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Political / Legal</strong></td><td>Political stability, regulation, trade barriers, incentives</td><td>Mixed</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="callout exam-tip">
        <div class="callout-title">Exam hint: quantitative vs qualitative factors</div>
        <p><strong>Quantitative factors</strong> can be put in numbers and compared directly: cost of land or rent, wage rates, transport costs, tax rates, distance to market or suppliers. <strong>Qualitative factors</strong> cannot be measured numerically and rely on judgement: workforce skill and attitude, quality of life, political stability, infrastructure quality, government attitude, ethical standards.</p>
        <p>Most STEEPLE factors carry both. Economic is weighted quantitative because land, rent and wage costs are numbers you can compare directly, but "labour skill" within it is a judgement call. Political/Legal mixes a hard number (a tax rate or tariff) with a judgement (how stable is that regime, really?). When a question asks you to evaluate a location decision, naming <em>which type</em> of factor you are using, and why it might be harder to verify or more likely to change, is worth more than just listing factors.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout remember">
        <div class="callout-title">Clustering</div>
        <p><strong>Clustering</strong> is when businesses in the same or related industries locate near each other, such as Saigon Hi-Tech Park's concentration of electronics manufacturers. It gives businesses access to a shared pool of skilled labour, nearby suppliers, and knowledge spillovers between firms. The trade-off is more local competition for staff, premises, and land, which can push up costs even as it lowers others.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Case studies: locating in Ho Chi Minh City</h2>
      <p>Three real location decisions, each weighted towards different STEEPLE factors.</p>

      <div class="content-card">
        <div class="content-card-title">Intel, Saigon Hi-Tech Park</div>
        <ul>
          <li>$1.5 billion plus invested over 20 years, on a 46.6 hectare site</li>
          <li>The largest assembly and test site in Intel's global network</li>
          <li>2,700 plus high-skilled technical workers</li>
          <li>Relocated key assembly and testing lines, partly from Costa Rica</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="font-size:0.85rem;"><a href="https://vir.com.vn/intel-relocates-key-assembly-and-testing-lines-to-ho-chi-minh-city-151966.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the article</a> (Vietnam Investment Review)</p>
      </div>

      <div class="content-card">
        <div class="content-card-title">Samsung's three Vietnam sites</div>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Bac Ninh (2008):</strong> close to Hanoi, Noi Bai Airport, and Hai Phong Port</li>
          <li><strong>Thai Nguyen (2013):</strong> 40 minutes from the Bac Ninh site, near Hanoi's universities</li>
          <li><strong>Ho Chi Minh City (2015):</strong> builds on southern Vietnam's existing plastic injection and moulding expertise, plus airport and seaport access</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="font-size:0.85rem;"><a href="https://viipip.com/homeen/?module=newsdetail&amp;newscode=9509" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the article</a> (VIIPIP)</p>
      </div>

      <div class="content-card">
        <div class="content-card-title">Uniqlo, Dong Khoi Street</div>
        <ul>
          <li>Flagship store, 3 floors, 3,000 plus square metres</li>
          <li>Arguably Vietnam's most prestigious retail street</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="font-size:0.85rem;"><a href="https://asia-prop.com/en/column/uniqlo-hochiminh-vietnam-dongkhoi-street/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the article</a></p>
      </div>

      <p>Ho Chi Minh City's own districts illustrate how location factors vary within one city:</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>District</th><th>Character</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>D1</strong></td><td>CBD: finance, premium retail, highest rents</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>D3</strong></td><td>About 16.6% cheaper than D1: SMEs, startups</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>D7, Phu My Hung</strong></td><td>High income, expats, luxury retail</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>D4</strong></td><td>Transforming, affordable, freelancers</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Tan Binh Industrial Park</strong></td><td>Garment, food and electronics manufacturing, near the airport</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Ethical, political and legal factors</h2>
      <div class="callout warning">
        <div class="callout-title">Location is also an ethical decision</div>
        <p>Low labour cost can tip into exploitation: a location decision is never purely financial. Governments compete for investment — Saigon Hi-Tech Park offers preferential tax and land-lease terms to attract Intel and other multinationals. Political stability makes costs and supply chains predictable, while a change in regulation or a new trade barrier can erase the cost benefit of a location overnight.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">International location</h2>
      <div class="callout remember">
        <div class="callout-title">Pending teacher review</div>
        <p>This section is AI-generated from lesson slides and is pending teacher review.</p>
      </div>
      <p>These four terms describe how a business reorganises <em>who</em> does its production and <em>where</em>.</p>
      <div class="key-terms">
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Outsourcing / subcontracting</div><div class="definition">Paying an external, independent business to carry out production or a business function that was previously done in-house. Can be domestic or international.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Insourcing</div><div class="definition">Bringing production or a function back in-house, using the business's own staff and resources, having previously been outsourced.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Offshoring</div><div class="definition">Moving production or a function to another country, whether kept in-house as an overseas subsidiary or contracted to an external supplier there.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Reshoring / onshoring</div><div class="definition">Moving production back to the business's home country after it had been located abroad.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout exam-tip">
        <div class="callout-title">Outsourcing vs offshoring: not the same axis</div>
        <p>Outsourcing is about <strong>who</strong> does the work: external supplier or in-house. Offshoring is about <strong>where</strong> it is done: which country. A business can do both simultaneously — Apple outsources iPhone assembly to Foxconn, and because Foxconn's relevant factories are mainly in China, that arrangement is also offshore.</p>
      </div>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Method</th><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Outsourcing</strong></td>
            <td>Lower cost from a specialist supplier's economies of scale and expertise; converts a fixed cost into a variable one; frees management to focus on core activities</td>
            <td>Less direct control over quality; dependency on a supplier's reliability; risk to intellectual property; possible domestic job losses and reputational risk</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Insourcing</strong></td>
            <td>Full control over quality and processes; protects intellectual property; more responsive when problems arise</td>
            <td>Needs the capital and skills to do it in-house; may raise unit cost if internal scale is small; loses the flexibility to switch supplier</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Offshoring</strong></td>
            <td>Access to lower labour costs, new markets or specialist skills; incentives from foreign governments</td>
            <td>Exposure to exchange rate risk; weaker legal protections in some locations; longer, less reliable supply chains; harder to monitor working conditions</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Reshoring / onshoring</strong></td>
            <td>Shorter, more reliable supply chains; easier quality control; "made in home country" positioning; avoids exchange rate and political risk abroad</td>
            <td>Higher domestic labour costs; may lose access to specialist offshore skills or suppliers; capital cost of rebuilding domestic capacity</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Key Terms</h2>
      <div class="key-terms">
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Location</div><div class="definition">The physical site where a business carries out its operations, chosen using factors such as STEEPLE.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Clustering</div><div class="definition">Businesses in the same or related industries locating near each other, sharing labour, suppliers and knowledge.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Outsourcing</div><div class="definition">Paying an external business to carry out production or a function previously done in-house.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Insourcing</div><div class="definition">Bringing a previously outsourced function back in-house.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Offshoring</div><div class="definition">Moving production or a function to another country, in-house or via a supplier.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Reshoring</div><div class="definition">Moving production back to the business's home country.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-title">Recap: what you should know</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Location is where operations happen; place is the marketing mix distribution decision — they can overlap, but are not the same</li>
        <li>Use STEEPLE to justify a location decision, weighing cost against quality-of-life, stability and ethical factors</li>
        <li>Clustering gives access to shared labour, suppliers and knowledge, at the cost of more local competition</li>
        <li>Outsourcing is about who does the work; offshoring is about where — a business can do both at once</li>
        <li>Insourcing and reshoring reverse outsourcing and offshoring respectively, trading cost for control and reliability</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /notes -->

  <!-- WORKED EXAMPLES -->
  <div id="worked" class="tab-content">

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Worked Example</div>

      <div class="callout remember">
        <div class="callout-title">Note</div>
        <p>The sample answer in this tab is pending teacher review.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Velora Motors</div>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Velora Motors is a mid-sized car manufacturer based in Germany. In 2024, Velora decided to outsource the production of its brake callipers to a specialist supplier in Poland rather than continue manufacturing them in-house. The decision was driven by rising labour costs at Velora's domestic factory and the supplier's ability to produce callipers at a lower unit cost using specialised equipment. However, six months after the switch, Velora received a higher than usual number of warranty claims linked to calliper defects. Investigations revealed that the Polish supplier had changed its quality control process without informing Velora, and Velora's own engineers no longer had direct oversight of the production line to catch the issue earlier.</p>
        <div class="exercise-q" style="margin-top:1rem;">Explain one advantage and one disadvantage of Velora Motors' change in the production of brake callipers. [4 marks]</div>
        <div class="answer-content" style="display:block;">
          <p><strong>The change:</strong> Velora outsourced (and offshored, since Poland is a different country) calliper production from its own German factory to an independent specialist supplier.</p>
          <p><strong>Advantage — lower unit cost.</strong> The Polish supplier can produce callipers more cheaply using specialised equipment, and Velora's own domestic labour costs were rising. Buying in at a lower unit cost than in-house production reduces Velora's cost of sales on every car built, which supports its margins or lets it price more competitively.</p>
          <p><strong>Disadvantage — loss of quality control.</strong> Once production moved outside the business, Velora's engineers no longer had direct oversight of the line. The supplier changed its quality control process without telling Velora, and defects were only caught after a spike in warranty claims. Outsourcing had removed Velora's ability to catch the problem early, which is now costing it warranty payouts and risking its reputation for reliability.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /worked -->

  <!-- PRACTICE -->
  <div id="practice" class="tab-content">

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 1: How well do you know HCMC?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">
          Draw a map of Ho Chi Minh City. Annotate it as the business map of the city — where would you expect different types of business to locate, and why? Generalisations are fine.
        </div>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Check your thinking</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">
            Intel: Saigon Hi-Tech Park, tech manufacturing.<br />
            Samsung: parts for household appliances.<br />
            Uniqlo: flagship on Dong Khoi Street, D1.<br />
            Tan Binh Industrial Park: garment, food and electronics manufacturing, near the airport.<br />
            D1: CBD, finance, premium retail, highest rents.<br />
            D3: about 16.6% cheaper than D1, SMEs, startups.<br />
            D7, Phu My Hung: high income, expats, luxury retail.<br />
            D4: transforming, affordable, freelancers.
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 2: Read the source, explain the why</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">
          Choose one or more of Intel, Samsung, and Uniqlo (see the Notes tab for links). Read the article and explain why the business is located there, referring to STEEPLE.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 3: Same business, different directions</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Solara Energy Components</div>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Solara Energy Components makes EV battery cells. You need one new factory. It needs rare battery engineers. It needs port/rail access for materials. It is hazardous: regulation is a threat, and there is possible local opposition. You want low costs, but no weaker protections than you think is fair. Government incentives are helpful, but not all governments are stable.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Task:</strong></p>
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Choose a country</li>
          <li>Choose a city</li>
          <li>Choose a site type (e.g. industrial park, edge of city near a port, redeveloped urban zone) — no real address needed, but it's neat if you can find one</li>
          <li>Justify using STEEPLE</li>
          <li>State which factors you prioritised, and which you traded off</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 4: Exit ticket</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">
          Explain two factors that a clothing retailer should consider when choosing a physical store location in Ho Chi Minh City. [4 marks]
        </div>
        <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--text-muted);">Write independently. Use two distinct STEEPLE factors, and explain the mechanism for each, not just a name-check.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 5: Define the terms</div>
      <p class="reveal-hint">Write your own definition first, in your own words, then reveal to check.</p>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="sort-grid">
          <div class="sort-item">Outsourcing / subcontracting<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Paying an external, independent business to do production or a function previously done in-house</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Insourcing<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Bringing a previously outsourced function back in-house</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Offshoring<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Moving production or a function to another country, in-house or via a supplier there</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Reshoring / onshoring<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Moving production back to the business's home country</span></button></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 6: Practice writing — Velora Motors</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">
          Write your own answer to the Velora Motors question below before checking the Worked Examples tab: state the change, then for both the advantage and the disadvantage, state it, explain it with reference to the case, and hammer it home with a specific detail from the case.
        </div>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">See the Worked Examples tab for the full case and a sample answer.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 7: Weigh it up</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">
          Before checking the Notes tab, sketch your own advantages/disadvantages table for outsourcing, insourcing, offshoring and onshoring.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 8: STEEPLE for in/off-shoring/sourcing</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">
          For each STEEPLE factor, name one thing that might encourage a company to outsource, insource, offshore or onshore. Each factor might act internally or externally.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 9: Make your own case study</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">
          Make a case study that shows the tension between in/off-shoring and in/out-sourcing.
        </div>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>A good case study:</strong></p>
        <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Has ambiguity — could be argued either way</li>
          <li>Has specific details: numbers, locations</li>
          <li>Provides context</li>
          <li>Has rich details that can blend with standard business theory, or even "break" it</li>
          <li>Is at least a little entertaining</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Stuck for an idea? Spin <a href="https://unisam.nz/newbiz" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unisam.nz/newbiz</a> once (and resist spinning again) — make your business about those words. Write 2–300 words.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Then write three questions based on your own case: a Define (2 marks), an Explain (4 marks), and a Discuss (10 marks). Sketch a mark scheme for each — what would a full-mark answer include? Share your case, then try one from a classmate.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /practice -->

